# The Search for a Leader: A Canto

Tamil title: ஆள் தேடு படலம்

"Bharatha, does Tiruvotriyur Shanmugam know astrology?" Veeran asked.

"What a strange question! He has no faith in astrology, yet you ask whether he knows it," I replied.

"I think so too. Whether or not he knows divination, he knows political prediction, and knows it well," said Veeran.

"Why do you say that?" I asked.

"After searching and searching for other leaders, wandering until exhausted, our friend Balasubramaniam declared that Periyar alone was fit to be the leader of Dravida Nadu. At the Salem conference, Comrade T. Shanmugam said, 'I do not know whether this opinion will remain or change. It must not change.' He knew what he was talking about. Balasubramaniam has shown his nature again, as in the proverb about the phantom returning to climb the drumstick tree. See how well Shanmugam knew political prediction!" Veeran explained.

It is true. Those who attended the Salem conference and saw the editor of the *Sunday Observer* that day would certainly have remembered Comrade T. Shanmugam's words and laughed when they saw the editor's posture after the conference.

With frightened eyes, a sweating face and a visibly dry throat, in a manner capable of winning the pity of many, he sang a *kattalai kalithurai*: "Long live Periyar! Long live the leader of Dravida! Long live Karl Marx!"

Just as his humility then made many laugh, the editor's heroic fury now makes many laugh. If Balasubramaniam imagines that anything he says or does will make someone in Tamilakam angry, he is mistaken. No one feels anger. Thinking of him produces only pity and laughter.

On the stage, I have seen the comic scene of a clown who angrily declares, "Will I let him go? I cannot! I shall show him!" and strides about swinging his arms and legs. Then he sees someone approaching, imagines it is the very man he threatened, collapses in fear, and begs, "No, no! I did nothing. Please forgive me."

Why should one grow angry with our friend Subramaniam? Laughter is only natural.

When stones and abuse rained upon the party, and those who had benefited from it donned saffron and fled indoors to bolt their doors, Periyar E. V. Ramasamy accepted the difficult task of conducting that party. Disregarding time and hardship, he worked and created a circle of support in the town.

If a person merely walked in Periyar's shadow, came to know four people, and then declared, "I shall reform the party, and my first task is to eliminate Periyar," could we say he was not mad simply because he was not confined in an asylum?

Even the tick lodged behind the ear of a bull pulling a heavy cart along a rough road imagines that the cart moves through its own power. Balasubramaniam likewise began to think that he alone could reform the party.

The "disease" grew severe. I believed he had received good "treatment" at Salem. Now it is clear that the patient's disease has not gone. Hence this "dose." Because he is my friend, I bear the responsibility of administering doses until he is cured.

He now displays this four-jewelled garland before the Sunday readership:

1. The Salem conference was not representative.
2. Periyar was not properly elected president of that conference.
3. An agreement was made on the night before the conference and was not honoured.
4. The resolutions at the conference were passed through demonstrations by Periyar's men.

If the first three claims are true, he could have spoken about them at the conference itself. Indeed, if the first two were true, he should not have attended at all.

Did this honourable man, who today says the conference was not representative, not know it then? Did he attend despite knowing it? Was that proper? Should not "intellectuals" like him attend only a properly constituted representative conference? Why did he come?

The Salem conference, he says, lacked representation and did not properly elect its president. Was it proper for him to attend such a conference? Having attended, did he at least open his mouth and say so? The editor is piloting an unmanned aircraft. Before the public that day, however, he spoke as the most ardent devotee.

He says an agreement was reached on the night before the conference. It was supposedly called the Gentlemen's Agreement and involved two parties. One side reportedly contained Comrades Pandian, C. G. Netto, advocate Ganesa Sankar, editor Balasubramaniam, editor Tirumalisami and A. Ponnambalanar. The other side contained Annadurai and his friends. These two sides supposedly discussed matters and reached an agreement, which, says the *Sunday Observer*, was then violated.

I believe Comrade Annadurai's article will clarify this matter.

Leaving that aside, only one course would have been sensible for the hero who returned home after failing to defeat the resolutions at the conference: he should have declared, "I shall no longer remain in this party."

Comrades who believed losing him would be equivalent to losing the party's heart should also have withdrawn and formed a new party. Even practical men would have done that, and people of judgment would call it the proper course. Only those who think they can pass their time through perpetual disorder choose a shortcut.

Our friend Balasubramaniam walks along that shortcut. I have told him to listen to me before he stumbles and falls.

Ten scenes have already been performed in the drama led by our friend. Four scenes remain, and with them the drama will end. He may have forgotten, so I remind him of the play:

1. The Canto of Furious Rage.
2. The Canto of Hiring Men for Wages.
3. The Canto of Carrying the Kavadi.
4. The Canto of the Wealthy Gentleman's Speech.
5. The Canto of the News from Salem.
6. The Canto of Hospitality.
7. The Canto of Bargaining.
8. The Canto of the Conference.
9. The Canto of Escaping with One's Head.
10. The Canto Behind the Curtain.

Those ten cantos have been completed. Four remain:

11. The Canto of Searching for a Leader.
12. The Canto of Carrying Tales to the Rulers.
13. The Canto of Dancing in Public.
14. The Canto of Destruction.

However quickly the editor wishes to stage them, the Dravidians stand ready to watch.

"There is a realm but no king. There is a party but no leader. A leader is urgently required," our friend P. P. has written. I am delighted. To assist him, I supply the following advertisement. It may be published in the next issue.

## Wanted! Wanted! Urgently Wanted!

A leader is urgently required for a party founded by heroes and now lying leaderless on the veranda of my office. Send applications immediately. They will be kept confidential.

### Qualifications

1. Must not mingle excessively with ordinary people and thereby lose prestige.

2. Must not conduct public meetings on beaches or open grounds. Meetings must be held only in large banquet halls.

3. Must provide a monthly sum for conducting English and Tamil daily newspapers.

4. Must bear the expenses of personnel and followers.

5. Must regularly and magnificently conduct garden parties, receptions for European gentlemen, dinners and tea parties.

6. Must be a hereditary zamindar or, failing that, a wealthy gentleman with a good income.

7. Must possess a disposition capable of embracing anyone. This rule applies only to men.

8. Must appoint speakers who denounce the Congress in fiery language; arrange for their words to be translated and sent to the government; and then establish a separate department to gather intelligence about the consequences.

9. Must offer "a good word" to those seeking employment, those desiring higher office and those wanting titles. Money for party development may be collected from them.

10. Must never, even by mistake, board a third-class railway carriage.

11. Must not visit remote villages and obscure corners.

12. Must not interfere in religious matters.

### Expected Benefits

1. Offices will be obtained.
2. Titles will be available.

When the advertisement "Leaders Wanted!" is published as shown above, the Canto of Searching for a Leader will begin. I eagerly await the auspicious day of its commencement.

*Dravida Nadu*, 10 September 1944.
